==Old English==

===Etymology===
From Germanic ''*haitan'' ‘command, name’, from Indo-European ''*kei-, *ki-'' ‘put in motion, be moving’.  Cognate with Old Frisian ''[[heta|hēta]]'', Old Saxon ''[[hetan|hētan]]'' (Dutch ''[[heten]]''), Old High German ''[[heizzan]]'' (German ''[[heißen]]'' ‘call, be called’), Old Norse ''[[heita]]'' (Swedish ''[[heta]]''), Gothic '''[[𐌷𐌰𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽]]'''.  The IE root is also the source of Ancient Greek '''{{polytonic|[[κίειν]]}}''' ‘put in motion’, Latin ''[[ciere]]'' ‘rouse, make active’.

===Pronunciation===
{{IPA|/ˈhɑ:tɑn/}}

===Verb===
{{Latinx|'''hātan'''}} (class VII strong: ''3 singular present'' {{Latinx|'''[[hætt|hǣtt]]'''}}, ''3 singular preterite'' '''[[het|hēt]]''', ''preterite plural'' '''[[heton|hēton]]''', ''past participle'' ('''ġe''')'''[[haten|hāten]]''')

# to [[order]], [[command]]
# to [[call]] or [[name]]
# {{intransitive|lang=ang}} to be called or named

